---
// The peer evaluation generator (#438). The rubric CSVs are read here, at
// build time, and every variant's survey is built once, so a CSV the
// generator cannot use fails the build. The component reads the roster in
// the browser only.
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import catmeCsv from "/canvas/assignments/peer-evaluation/catme-rubric.csv?raw";
import peerCsv from "/canvas/assignments/peer-evaluation/peer-evaluation-rubric.csv?raw";
import PeerEvaluationGenerator from "../../components/tools/PeerEvaluationGenerator.svelte";
import PeerEvaluationScorer from "../../components/tools/PeerEvaluationScorer.svelte";
import { variants } from "../../data/peer-evaluation.mjs";
import { parseRubricCsv } from "../../lib/rubric-csv.mjs";
import {
  NON_COMPLETION_SCORE,
  PAIR_DIVERGENCE,
  PAIR_LOW_AVERAGE,
  PAIR_SELF_RANGE,
} from "../../lib/tools/peer-score.mjs";
import { buildPeerSurvey } from "../../lib/tools/peer-survey-qsf.mjs";

const byInstrument = {
  catme: parseRubricCsv(catmeCsv, "catme-rubric.csv"),
  regular: parseRubricCsv(peerCsv, "peer-evaluation-rubric.csv"),
};
const rubrics = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(variants).map(([key, { instrument }]) => [
    key,
    byInstrument[instrument as keyof typeof byInstrument],
  ])
);
for (const [variant, rubric] of Object.entries(rubrics)) {
  buildPeerSurvey({ rubric, variant });
}
// The scorer (#6) scores the regular survey against the same parse.
const peerRubric = byInstrument.regular;
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    description:
      "Generate the peer evaluation survey and its contact list from the Canvas roster.",
    editUrl: false,
    head: [{ attrs: { content: "noindex", name: "robots" }, tag: "meta" }],
    pagefind: false,
    title: "Peer Evaluation Survey",
  }}
>
  <p>
    Drop the Canvas roster with groups to get the Qualtrics survey and the
    contact list. The file is read in your browser and never uploaded.
  </p>

  <PeerEvaluationGenerator client:only="svelte" {rubrics} />

  <h2>Where the inputs come from and what to do next</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      In Canvas, People, Groups: export the group set's roster with groups as
      CSV (<code>name, canvas_user_id, user_id, login_id, sections,
      group_name, canvas_group_id, group_id</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      Drop it above, pick the survey and the course and term, and download
      both files. Students alone on a team or in no team are listed above and
      left out; a team over 10 stops the run.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Qualtrics, Create a new project, Survey, From a file: import the
      <code>.qsf</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In the directory, create a mailing list from the contact-list CSV. Every
      column maps by its header: <code>Email</code>, <code>Team</code>,
      <code>TeamSize</code>, <code>SelfFloor</code>, and
      <code>Team Member 1</code> to <code>9</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Set the availability dates on the distribution, not in the survey.
    </li>
    <li>
      Distribute by email with personal links only. The survey is by invitation,
      so the anonymous link does not open it.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If the Loop &amp; Merge version fails to import or the staff test shows a
    wrong page, regenerate with "One block per teammate slot": same
    questions, one block per slot behind a branch.
  </p>

  <h2>Score the export</h2>
  <p>
    Drop the three files, check the survey type, and Run. The files are read
    in your browser and never uploaded. CATME scoring (spring end-of-term) is
    not available yet.
  </p>

  <PeerEvaluationScorer client:only="svelte" rubric={peerRubric} />

  <h3>Where the inputs come from</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Qualtrics export.</strong> In the survey, Data &amp; Analysis,
      Export &amp; Import, Export Data: CSV, Download all fields, and
      "Use choice text" on. Labels exports only: a values export (numeric
      codes) is refused. Keep the three header rows; the page skips them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Roster with groups.</strong> The same Canvas People, Groups
      export the generator reads. It decides who is on which team and N, the
      team size.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rubric assessment.</strong> In Canvas, the peer evaluation
      assignment the grade goes to (midterm or end-of-term), its rubric,
      export the assessments as CSV: <code>Student Id, Student Name</code>,
      then <code>&lt;criterion&gt; - Rating, - Points, - Comments</code> per
      criterion. The page fills it and you import it back on the same
      assignment.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>What Run does</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Reads the export's columns by their tags and ImportIds: the four
      ratings, the comment, and the hidden ratee on each looped page, the
      split, and the embedded <code>Team</code>, <code>TeamSize</code>,
      <code>SelfFloor</code>, and <code>Team Member 1</code> to <code>9</code>.
      It names the survey type and stops on a header it cannot place.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keeps responses with <code>Finished</code> true, previews only when the
      box is checked, and the latest per
      <code>RecipientEmail</code> by <code>RecordedDate</code>. A
      <code>RecordedDate</code> not written as Qualtrics writes it stops the
      run: export again rather than re-saving the file in a spreadsheet.
    </li>
    <li>
      Names whom each page rated twice: the hidden ratee answer, and the loop
      position (page 1 is the respondent, page N is
      <code>Team Member N-1</code>). When they disagree, or the ratee is not
      on the roster team, the page is reported and left out; it is never
      guessed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Checks the split totals 100. A self share below the floor, computed
      from the roster's N, is raised to it and the rater's other shares
      scaled to keep 100. Nothing is deducted.
    </li>
    <li>
      Scores each student from what the teammates gave them, as the
      <a href="/assignments/peer-evaluations/#grade-calculation">Grade
      Calculation</a> specifies. A student with no finished response scores
      {NON_COMPLETION_SCORE}. A student nobody rated gets no score and is
      reported.
    </li>
    <li>
      Flags, never changing a score: on a team of two, a self share outside
      {PAIR_SELF_RANGE[0]} to {PAIR_SELF_RANGE[1]}, ratings received averaging
      below {PAIR_LOW_AVERAGE}, and two partners whose ratings of each other
      differ by {PAIR_DIVERGENCE} or more. The self-versus-peer gap
      table lists everyone, largest first; its share gap uses the raw self
      share, before any rescale.
    </li>
    <li>
      Writes each rubric criterion's points (score times its points, divided
      by 100) and the highest rating at or below those points, matched to
      the export's columns by criterion name, with the names and points read
      from the peer evaluation rubric CSV. A non-completer gets a
      fifth of {NON_COMPLETION_SCORE} on every criterion (below the point
      distribution's lowest band), totalling {NON_COMPLETION_SCORE}, with a
      note.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3>The downloads</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rubric assessment</strong>: import it on the assignment's
      rubric. Rows left as exported are named in the report; grade them by
      hand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Feedback for students</strong>: per criterion, the mean rating
      received and its score, the normalized share, and the peer score. Means
      only, no comments. On a team of two the mean is the one teammate's
      rating.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Details</strong> and <strong>comments</strong>: instructor only.
      Never send them to students.
    </li>
  </ul>
</StarlightPage>
